  "final_text": "All four steps are done, and I read the dashboard and the rule back to confirm them.\n\n**1. Data view: 'Kibana Sample Data Flights'** (id `d3d7af60-4c81-11e8-b3d7-01146121b73d`, index `kibana_sample_data_flights`). Its time field is **`timestamp`**. Its fields are:\n- **Date:** `timestamp`\n- **Keyword:** `Carrier`, `Dest`, `DestAirportID`, `DestCityName`, `DestCountry`, `DestRegion`, `DestWeather`, `FlightDelayType`, `FlightNum`, `FlightTimeHour`, `Origin`, `OriginAirportID`, `OriginCityName`, `OriginCountry`, `OriginRegion`, `OriginWeather`\n- **Number:** `AvgTicketPrice`, `DistanceKilometers`, `DistanceMiles`, `FlightDelayMin`, `FlightTimeMin`, `dayOfWeek`, `hour_of_day`\n- **Boolean:** `Cancelled`, `FlightDelay`\n- **Geo point:** `DestLocation`, `OriginLocation`\n- **Metadata:** `_id`, `_index`, `_score`, `_source`, `_ignored`\n\n**2. Dashboard 'S4-6380d8a2 Flights'** (id `f7c5b3c2-415b-4469-971c-6e4bbc64a152`) has exactly two panels, both using that data view:\n- \"Flights over time\": a line chart counting flights over `timestamp`.\n- A bar chart counting flights for each `Carrier`, showing the top 10.\n\n**3. Rule 'S4-6380d8a2 Cancelled flights'** (id `d4faf22d-283f-4038-b90e-3c44fe5a3140`) is an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_flights`. It runs every minute, matches documents where `Cancelled` is true, and fires when the count over the last 5 minutes is greater than 10. \"Arrive\" is measured by `timestamp`, the data's only time field. The rule was enabled as soon as it was created, so I didn't need to enable it. It has no actions attached, so it records alerts but doesn't send notifications.\n\n**4.** I renamed the carrier panel to \"Flights by carrier\", and reading the dashboard back shows the new title. Reading the rule back shows `enabled: true`.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
